zombies on request, so a freed object says which one it was

The zombie machinery is compiled in here, ENABLE_ZOMBIES is defined at the top of this
file, and nothing ever called _CFEnableZombies. So a message to a freed object crashed
inside objc_class::demangledName naming a garbage class, and the selector and the class
were both lost, which is the one thing that would have identified it.

___forwarding___ already prints the original class and selector when the receiver is a
zombie. It only needed switching on. NSZombieEnabled does that, read once at CF
initialisation. Present but EMPTY is off, and so is 0 or n, because a shell writes VAR=
for an unset variable and nobody means that as yes.

--- a/CFRuntime.c
+++ b/CFRuntime.c
@@ -982,6 +982,24 @@
         __CFTSDLinuxInitialize();
 #endif
         
+        /*
+         * ZOMBIES ON REQUEST. The machinery is compiled in and nothing ever switched it on, so a
+         * message to a freed object crashed inside objc naming a garbage class instead of saying
+         * which object and which selector. ___forwarding___ already prints exactly that when the
+         * receiver is a zombie.
+         *
+         * Present but EMPTY is off, which is what a shell writes for VAR= and not what anyone
+         * means by it. Off by default: zombies never free anything.
+         */
+        {
+            const char *zombies = getenv("NSZombieEnabled");
+
+            if (zombies != NULL && zombies[0] != '\0' && zombies[0] != '0'
+                && zombies[0] != 'n' && zombies[0] != 'N') {
+                _CFEnableZombies();
+            }
+        }
+
         __CFProphylacticAutofsAccess = true;
 
         for (CFIndex idx = 0; idx < sizeof(__CFEnv) / sizeof(__CFEnv[0]); idx++) {
